Building the book
=================

This file explains how to build the book, run its checks and run the tests
of its tools. Run every command from the root of the repository.


The environment
---------------

Nix pins every tool that the book uses. flake.nix names the tools, and
flake.lock records the release of nixpkgs that they come from. So every
machine uses the same versions: Python 3.13, Sphinx 9.1, Verilator 5.048,
Icarus Verilog 13.0, yosys 0.62, SymbiYosys 0.61, Yices 2.7.0, z3 4.16,
radon 6.0.1, nextpnr 0.10 with ecppack of trellis for the ECP5, and TeX Live
2025.

1. Install Nix. The instructions are at https://nixos.org/download.
2. Run ./dev to open a shell in the environment.
3. Or run ./dev followed by one command, for example: ./dev make check

The first run of ./dev puts about 2.1 GiB of tools into /nix/store (the size
that nix path-info -S gives for the environment). Later runs take a few
seconds. ./dev also sets the Git configuration core.hooksPath to tools/hooks,
so that the pre-push hook runs.

A Nix from a distribution package, such as the nix package of Arch Linux or
CachyOS, runs each build through a daemon (a background service), nix-daemon.
Such a package can leave out the store, /nix/store. If the store does not
exist, ./dev stops with the message "the Nix store /nix/store does not exist".
If you see this message, do these steps:

1. Create the store with the group and mode that the Nix installer gives it:

       sudo install -d -m 0755 /nix
       sudo install -d -g nixbld -m 1775 /nix/store

2. Start the daemon, and start it at each boot:

       sudo systemctl enable --now nix-daemon.socket

3. Add your user to the group nix-users, which can reach the daemon:

       sudo usermod -aG nix-users "$USER"

4. Log out and log in again, so that the new group applies. Then run ./dev.

The names nixbld and nix-users are those of the Arch Linux package. If your
distribution uses other names, use its names.

Outside the environment, make stops with the message "run ./dev make
<target>". If you see this message, run the command that it names.


The make targets
----------------

make check    Makes sure that the book obeys the rules of
              book/doc/doc.rst, and translates each twin (the model of a
              rule) with tools/twin.py. Lints each Verilog file with
              Verilator and reads it with yosys. Then it runs each check
              directive with tools/run_checks.py. It passes only when it
              reports 0 findings (breaks of a rule) and no check fails.
make weave    Builds the reader edition: build/html/index.html and
              build/latex/bcw2.pdf. It ends with an index of code, which
              links each file and each fragment to the block that defines
              it and to each block that uses it. It reports findings but
              does not stop on them.
make test     Runs the tests of the tools, in tools/tests, with pytest on
              each core of the machine.
make tangle   Writes the code of the book into build/: the Verilog into
              build/rtl, and the value of each PARAMETER into
              build/rtl/bcw_params.sv. A twin is not a file: the tangle
              writes its code into build/checks.json for each equiv check.
              It writes the code of each check into build/checks, and a list
              of the checks and the value of each PARAMETER into
              build/checks.json. It also writes build/tangle.json, with the
              chapter line of each line that it writes and a hash of each
              file. If a file in build/ changed after the last tangle, make
              tangle keeps the file and reports it, so that no edit is lost.
              Delete the file to tangle it again. A file of the last tangle
              that the book no longer tangles is deleted, unless it changed
              after the last tangle: then make tangle keeps it and reports
              it too.
make clean    Removes build/.

Run make check after each edit to the book. Run make test after each edit
to a tool.


Reading a finding
-----------------

make check prints each finding in this form:

    book/core/core.rst:50: WARNING: [stamps] core.rotation: the rule's English changed since its twin was last read
        fix: read the twin against the rule, then set :stamp: 75abc2dc

The first part is the line of the chapter. The name in brackets is the rule
of book/doc/doc.rst that the text breaks. The anchor after it names the
chunk (the labelled block of text) that breaks the rule. The second line
tells you how to fix the finding.

If docutils cannot read a directive, it reports an ERROR on the directive
line and leaves the directive out. The book then misses the anchors, terms and
code of that directive, so make check runs no other check and make tangle
writes nothing. make check prints:

    WARNING: bcw: 1 error in the chapters, so no check ran: correct the errors first

Correct the errors, then run make check again.

Verilator and the twins report locations in the files of build/.
tools/linemap.py changes each of these locations to the line of the chapter
that holds the code. It reads build/tangle.json, which make tangle writes with
the chapter line of each line of each tangled file. So each location that make
check prints is a line in book/.


Fragments
---------

A source directive whose argument is a colon and a name, such as
:core.pair-logic, defines a fragment. The colon sets a fragment apart from an
anchor. A line that holds only <<:core.pair-logic>> in another source
directive uses the fragment. make tangle puts the
fragment in place of that line, with the indentation of the line:

    .. source:: build/rtl/core/pair.v

       module pair (input wire a, output wire b);
           <<:core.pair-logic>>
       endmodule

    .. source:: :core.pair-logic

       assign b = a;

So the code of a rule can stand next to the rule, and a skeleton elsewhere can
put a module in order. Each fragment and each file has one code block
(doc.one-block), so code from another block joins a file only through a
fragment use. A twin cannot use a fragment, because a fragment use is not in
the twin language (doc.twin-forms). The rules of fragments are in the section
Fragments of book/doc/doc.rst.


Checks
------

A check directive names its kind in its argument: test, prove or equiv. Its
option :verifies: lists the REQUIREMENTs that it checks:

    .. check:: equiv
       :verifies: core.rotation
       :module: core_rotate

A test holds a SystemVerilog testbench. A prove holds SystemVerilog
properties, and its option :depth: sets the number of steps of the proof (20
if the option is not there). An equiv holds no code. Its option :module:
names a Verilog module, and its option :twin: names the function of the twin
that the module must equal (the module name if the option is not there).

Each REQUIREMENT must appear in the :verifies: list of a check or in an
"# implements:" comment in tools/, or carry the option :verify: none
(doc.verified). make tangle writes the code of each check into
build/checks/<name>.sv. The name is the first anchor of :verifies: and the
kind, such as core.rotation.test, with -2, -3 and so on for a later check of
the same name. build/checks.json lists each check with its kind, its options,
its file and its chapter line.

make check runs each check with tools/run_checks.py, in the chapter order.
The first module in the code of a test or a prove is its top module. Each
check reads build/rtl/bcw_params.sv and each Verilog file of build/rtl. A
test passes when Verilator builds the testbench and the testbench exits 0,
so $fatal fails it. A prove passes when SymbiYosys proves each assertion
with the smtbmc engine and Yices. The work of each check is in
build/run/<name>.

An equiv passes when z3 proves that the module equals its twin for every
value of the input ports. yosys writes the module as SMT. tools/twin.py
translates the function of the twin to z3 terms. The function takes an
argument for each input port, and returns a dict with a value for each
output port. If an output of the module differs from the value of the twin
for one input, the runner prints that input and both outputs:

    book/core/core.rst:82: FAIL: [check] core.rotation.equiv: the module and the twin differ
        turn=7: module next=0, twin next=8

An equiv fails for a module that holds state (a register or a memory).
Prove such a module with a prove check.

The runner prints each result at the chapter line of its check directive.
After a failure come the lines of the tool that tell why, with each location
mapped to its chapter line:

    book/core/core.rst:91: FAIL: [check] core.rotation.test: the testbench stopped with exit 1
        %Error: book/core/core.rst:101: Verilog $stop

The source lines that Verilator quotes under an error keep the line numbers
of the file in build/checks.


The twin language
-----------------

The tools never run a twin as Python. tools/twin.py reads the syntax of a
twin and translates each form to a z3 term, so "0 if turn == 7 else turn + 1"
means what a reader of Python expects. make check reports each form outside
the language at its chapter line (doc.twin-forms). The language is:

- A twin holds only function definitions, without decorators, default
  values or keyword arguments, and without imports.
- A function holds lines of the form "name = value", then one return. The
  function that an equiv check names returns a dict of the output ports.
- An integer is a literal, an argument, a local name or the constant name
  of a PARAMETER, such as CORE_THREADS. The operators are + - * and unary -,
  ** by a constant of 0 or more, // and % by a positive constant, << and >>
  by a constant of 0 or more, and ~ & | ^. The functions are min, max, abs
  and the other functions of the twin, which take integers and cannot call
  themselves.
- A condition is a comparison, a chain of comparisons, and, or, not, True
  or False. "a if c else b" needs a condition c.

A twin cannot use a loop, a dict, a list, a string, a float, "in", or a call
of any other function. Each integer is exact: the translation gives each
value enough bits that no value overflows, as in Python. A twin that needs
more than 256 bits fails its equiv check.


The tools
---------

tools/bcw.py        The Sphinx extension. It reads the chapters, applies
                    the rules to them and tangles the code.
tools/weave.py      The Sphinx extension that builds the reader edition.
tools/linemap.py    Maps a location in build/ to its chapter line.
tools/run_checks.py Runs each check directive of the book.
tools/twin.py       Translates a twin in the twin language to z3.
tools/metrics.py    Prints the lines of code, McCabe and Halstead metrics of
                    the code in tools/, without its tests:
                    ./dev python3 tools/metrics.py
tools/ste_lint.py   The linter of plain English that doc.linter uses.
tools/conf.py       The Sphinx configuration of the book.
tools/hooks/        The Git pre-push hook.
tools/tests/        The tests of the tools.


The tests
---------

pytest runs the tests, and pytest.ini holds its configuration. Each test
builds a small book in a temporary folder. It then compares the findings,
the tangled files or the woven pages with the expected result. The helpers
that build a book, such as Book and the chapter GOOD, are in
tools/tests/book.py. Some tests run Verilator, Icarus Verilog, SymbiYosys or
latexmk, so run the tests inside the environment:

    ./dev make test

To run one class of tests, name its file and class:

    ./dev pytest tools/tests/test_bcw_rules.py::TargetTest

To run the tests whose names hold a word, give the word after -k:

    ./dev pytest -k boxs

A test that runs over a list of cases runs each case as a test of its own,
with the case in brackets after the name of the test.

Most tests of the rules are cases, which are data and not code. Each file of
tools/tests/cases holds the cases of one or more rules, in TOML. A case edits
GOOD and lists the findings of the edited chapter. It names the line of each
finding by a text that the line holds:

    [[case]]
    name = "an unknown parent is a finding on the option line"
    findings = [
        {check = "references", anchor = "core.turn", at = ":parent: core.nothing"},
    ]

    [[case.replace]]
    old = "   :parent: core.core\n"
    new = "   :parent: core.nothing\n"

tools/tests/test_cases.py runs each case, and its docstring lists the keys of
a case. To test a rule, add a case. Write a test in Python only if it must
read more than the findings of one chapter, such as a message, the tangled
files or the woven pages.

The reference outputs in tools/tests/golden are the HTML pages and the
LaTeX of one book that holds each feature of the weave. A test compares the
weave of that book with them. If you change the weave on purpose, rewrite
them, then read their difference in git before you commit it:

    ./dev pytest --update-golden tools/tests/test_weave.py
    git diff tools/tests/golden

A new check needs a test that fails before the check exists and passes
after it. A new rule in book/doc/doc.rst needs a check in tools/ that names
the rule in an "# implements:" comment. It also needs a test that names the
rule: a case with a finding of its check, or a test class whose docstring
names the rule. A test in tools/tests/test_cases.py makes sure of that.


Before you push
---------------

The pre-push hook runs make test and make check on each commit that a push
sends. It runs them in a worktree (a second checkout of one commit), through
the ./dev of that commit. So each commit runs in the environment that it
pins, and edits that are not committed play no part. If either target
fails, the hook stops the push. A push takes about 30 seconds.


Changing a tool
---------------

To add a tool, add its nixpkgs package to the list "packages" in flake.nix.

To move to a newer release of nixpkgs:

1. Open https://channels.nixos.org/nixos-26.05, or the channel of a later
   release. The address redirects to the folder of the newest release.
2. In flake.nix, set the URL of the input nixpkgs to the file
   nixexprs.tar.xz in that folder.
3. Run: nix --extra-experimental-features 'nix-command flakes' flake update
4. Run ./dev make test, ./dev make check and ./dev make weave. Fix each
   failure that the new versions cause, then commit flake.nix and
   flake.lock together.


Messages that do no harm
------------------------

"warning: Git tree '...' is dirty"
    Nix prints this when the repository holds edits that are not committed.
